          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A 50-year-old woman has cervical intraepithelial neoplasia grade III (CIN III) w</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 50-year-old woman has cervical intraepithelial neoplasia grade III (CIN III) with cells showing malignant cytology. Why is this lesion unable to metastasise?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>The basement membrane remains intact, blocking access to lymphatics and blood vessels</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. By definition, carcinoma in situ has not breached the basement membrane. Without access to lymphovascular channels, the five-step metastatic cascade cannot begin.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>CIN III cells lack the mutations needed for vascular invasion</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>CIN III cells carry malignant mutations and malignant cytology. The barrier to metastasis is physical — the intact basement membrane — not a lack of genetic change.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>The cervical epithelium lacks lymphatic drainage pathways</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The cervix has extensive lymphatic drainage. CIN III cannot metastasise because the intact basement membrane prevents tumour cells from reaching these channels.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>The immune system destroys all CIN III cells that attempt to enter the bloodstream</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>While immune surveillance can eliminate circulating tumour cells, the fundamental reason in situ lesions cannot metastasise is that they have not breached the basement membrane.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The answer is the intact basement membrane. Carcinoma in situ, by definition, has not breached this barrier. Once breached, tumour cells gain access to lymphatics and vessels, and the metastatic cascade begins."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Exactly. The basement membrane is the critical barrier: breach it, and the five-step metastatic cascade — invasion, intravasation, circulation, extravasation, colonisation — can begin."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
                "enableSolutionsButton": true,
                "enableCheckButton": true,
