          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A farmer in rural India develops hepatocellular carcinoma without any history of hepatitis or alcohol use. He regularly consumes grain stored in humid conditions. Which carcinogen and molecular target best explain this scenario?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Benzo-a-pyrene targeting the RAS gene</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Benzo-a-pyrene is a tobacco-associated carcinogen activated by CYP1A1 to damage lung DNA. It is not linked to contaminated grain or liver carcinoma.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Aflatoxin B1 targeting codon 249 of TP53</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Aspergillus moulds colonise grain stored in humid conditions and produce aflatoxin B1, which specifically targets codon 249 of TP53 in hepatocytes, driving hepatocellular carcinoma.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Ultraviolet radiation causing pyrimidine dimers</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>UV radiation causes skin cancers through pyrimidine dimer formation and is normally repaired by nucleotide excision repair. It is unrelated to grain contamination or liver carcinoma.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Helicobacter pylori driving chronic inflammation</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>H. pylori is associated with gastric carcinoma through chronic antigen-driven inflammation, not with hepatocellular carcinoma or contaminated grain.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The answer is aflatoxin B1. Produced by Aspergillus moulds on grain stored in humid conditions, it targets codon 249 of TP53 in the liver. This is a clinically important chemical carcinogen in tropical regions."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. Aflatoxin B1 from Aspergillus mould is a major cause of hepatocellular carcinoma in tropical regions where grain is stored in humid conditions — a direct application of the initiation–promotion model."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
