          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A pathology report describes a tumour as Grade 3, Stage I. What does this combin</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A pathology report describes a tumour as Grade 3, Stage I. What does this combination indicate about the tumour?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>The tumour is well-differentiated and has spread to regional lymph nodes</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Grade 3 indicates poor differentiation, not well-differentiated. Stage I means localised disease without lymph node involvement.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>The tumour is poorly differentiated and has already metastasised to distant sites</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>While Grade 3 correctly indicates poor differentiation, Stage I means the tumour is localised. Distant metastasis would correspond to Stage IV in the TNM system.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>The tumour is poorly differentiated but remains localised to the primary site</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Grading and staging are independent assessments. Grade 3 reflects poor differentiation, while Stage I indicates the tumour has not spread beyond the primary site — such a tumour may still be curable.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>The tumour is moderately differentiated and confined to the organ of origin</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Grade 3 indicates poor differentiation, not moderate (which would be Grade 2). Only the staging part — localised disease — is correctly described here.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Grading and staging are independent. Grade 3 = poorly differentiated (how abnormal the cells look). Stage I = localised (how far the tumour has spread). Both assessments are needed to guide treatment planning."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. Grading rates differentiation (Grade 1–4) while staging maps spread (TNM). They are independent — a high-grade tumour at Stage I has not yet spread and may still be curable with appropriate treatment."
                }
              ],
